docs/security.md            # Phoenix Print Farm — Security Architecture (2026)

This document defines the security architecture of the Phoenix Print Farm.
It describes how physical systems, digital assets, production workflows,
and governance controls are protected against compromise, tampering,
theft, and external interference.

Phoenix security is **preventive, deterministic, and sovereign**.

---

## 1. Security Philosophy

Traditional manufacturing security relies on:
- Network perimeter defenses
- Vendor-managed platforms
- Reactive incident response

Phoenix replaces this with:
- Zero-trust execution
- Cryptographic authorization
- Local control by default
- No implicit trust between components

If a system is not explicitly authorized, it does not operate.

---

## 2. Threat Model

Phoenix is designed to withstand:

- Supply chain disruption
- IP theft and file exfiltration
- Insider misuse
- Remote disablement attempts
- Network compromise
- Vendor backdoors
- Political or economic coercion

Security assumes **hostile conditions**, not normal operation.

---

## 3. Physical Security Layer

### 3.1 Print Cell Security
- Controlled physical access to print cells
- Tamper-evident enclosures
- Hardware identification per printer and robot

### 3.2 Facility Controls
- Zoned access (operators, maintenance, auditors)
- Local surveillance and logging
- Offline operation capability

Physical compromise does not grant digital authority.

---

## 4. Digital Asset Security

### 4.1 Encrypted Design Files
- Files encrypted at rest and in transit
- Decryption occurs only inside authorized print cells
- No plaintext storage outside execution environment

### 4.2 Key Management
- Keys bound to jurisdiction and role
- No shared master keys
- Revocation supported without system downtime

Loss of a key does not expose the system.

---

## 5. Execution Security

### 5.1 Authorized Runtime Only
- Print jobs execute only if policy permits
- Files cannot run outside approved environments
- Unauthorized files fail deterministically

### 5.2 No General-Purpose Execution
- Print systems do not function as open compute nodes
- No arbitrary code execution
- Execution scope limited to manufacturing tasks

---

## 6. Network Security

### 6.1 Zero-Trust Networking
- Every component authenticated
- No implicit trust based on location
- Encrypted internal communications

### 6.2 Air-Gapped Operation
- Full functionality without internet access
- Optional periodic synchronization
- No hard dependency on external services

### 6.3 Segmentation
- Control plane isolated from print cells
- Monitoring systems read-only where possible
- Lateral movement prevented by design

---

## 7. Supply Chain Security

- Materials authenticated at intake
- Material profiles bound to approved applications
- Unauthorized materials rejected by system policy

Digital and physical supply chains are independently verified.

---

## 8. Audit & Forensics

- Immutable logs for all actions
- Cryptographically signed events
- Local retention with controlled export

Security incidents are:
- Detectable
- Traceable
- Provable

No silent failure modes exist.

---

## 9. Update & Patch Security

- Updates require governance approval
- All updates are signed and verified
- Rollback supported
- No forced remote updates

Security updates cannot override sovereignty guarantees.

---

## 10. Insider Risk Mitigation

- Role-based access control
- Least-privilege enforcement
- Separation of duties
- Continuous behavioral logging

No single operator can compromise the system.

---

## 11. External Dependency Exclusion

Phoenix explicitly excludes:
- Mandatory foreign cloud services
- External DRM enforcement
- Vendor-controlled license servers
- Remote kill switches

Security authority remains local at all times.

---

## 12. Failure & Compromise Containment

If compromise is detected:
- Affected components isolate automatically
- Production continues elsewhere if permitted
- No cascading failure across the farm

Containment is automatic, not manual.

---

## 13. Summary

Phoenix Print Farm security provides:

- Deterministic protection of IP
- Sovereign control of execution
- Resistance to external interference
- Provable compliance and integrity

Security is not an add-on —
it is a **structural property** of the system.
